{color.GG}LLSI {color.WW}(Loadable Library Shell Initialization) {color.N}is a runtime loading system that exposes registered functions and variables to the global shell environment.
It provides flexible framework-wide functionality for scripting, but should be used carefully due to its global scope.

{color.YY}Warning{color.N}:
    {color.R}- {color.WW}LLSI modifies the global shell environment.{color.N}
    {color.R}- {color.WW}Improper use may cause function or variable conflicts.{color.N}
    {color.R}- {color.WW}Recommended only for framework internals and advanced scripting.{color.N}
    {color.R}- {color.BB}init/llsi.init {color.WW}does not support comment syntax of any kind.{color.N}

Example {color.GG}init/llsi.init {color.N}syntax:
{color.R}your{color.GG}::{color.R}lib{color.GG}::{color.R}name {color.N}{
    key {color.GG}-> {color.CC}source{color.N}
    key {color.GG}-> {color.CC}source{color.N}
}

Real Example:
{color.R}your{color.GG}::{color.R}lib{color.GG}::{color.R}name {color.N}{
    runtime.{color.BB}init {color.GG}-> {color.CC}lib/shell/myinit.init{color.N}
    runtime.{color.BB}free {color.GG}-> {color.CC}lib/shell/myfree.init{color.N}
}

Ensure the source file includes `{color.PP}readonly {color.GG}-{color.N}f {color.R}yourfunc{color.N}` and `{color.PP}export {color.GG}-{color.N}f {color.R}yourfunc{color.N}`.
For reliability and security, exported functions should also be marked readonly.
This prevents accidental or intentional overrides by other sources.
Without export, functions will not be available for use in scripts.

For variables:
{color.PP}export {color.R}myval{color.GG}={color.N}val; {color.PP}readonly {color.R}myval{color.N}